<mjml lang="en">
  <mj-head>
    <!-- en-tools-config {
      "spacingScale": { "None": 0, "Half": 8, "Single": 16, "Double": 32, "Triple": 48, "Quadruple": 64 },
      "classSpacingScales": {
        "caption": { "None": 0, "Quarter": 4, "Half": 8, "Single": 16, "Double": 32, "Triple": 48, "Quadruple": 64 },
        "hero-reveal": { "None": 0, "Half": 8, "Single": 16, "Double": 32, "Triple": 48, "Quadruple": 64, "100": 100, "150": 150, "200": 200, "250": 250, "300": 300, "350": 350, "400": 400, "450": 450, "500": 500 }
      },
      "widthPresets": { "Full Bleed": 0, "Single": 16, "Double": 32, "Triple": 48, "Quadruple": 64 },
      "geometryReachPx": 64,
      "brandColors": { "Snow": "#F5FAF1", "Moss": "#CEE4C5", "Earth": "#362229", "Evergreen": "#006837", "Fern": "#39B54A", "Grass": "#8CC63F", "Sky": "#5DD8D8", "Sun": "#F7931E", "Light Grey": "#FAFAFA" }
    } -->
    <!--
      BLOCKS UNDER REPAIR — not a catalog page, not shipped.

      A holding pen for blocks pulled out of main.mjml while something about
      them is unresolved. It carries main's head verbatim (the en-tools-config,
      the builder-band sheet, styles.css) so a block behaves here exactly as it
      did there — the point is to look at it in isolation, not in a different
      environment.

      Moving a block here has three consequences worth knowing:
        * it stops exporting, so EN never sees it;
        * its versions.json entity disappears — version-sync hashes blocks from
          main.mjml alone — so returning it later restarts its version at v1;
        * it is still a compiled page, so every check-catalog guard still
          applies to it. That is deliberate: a block under repair is exactly
          the one you want linted.

      Put a block back by moving its START/END region into main.mjml under the
      right Category band. When this file is empty, delete it.
    -->
    <mj-breakpoint width="600px" />
    <mj-title>TPL — Blocks under repair</mj-title>
    <!-- No mj-preview: EN injects its own hidden preheader from each email's Preview Text setting (measured 2026-08-10) — a template-baked one would double up in inbox snippets. Autoresponder sources (donation-thank-you) keep theirs; they do not go out through Marketing Tools broadcasts. -->

    <!-- Baseline everything inherits -->
    <mj-attributes>
      <mj-text
        color="#000000"
        font-size="18px"
        font-weight="400"
        font-family="Tahoma, sans-serif"
        line-height="24px"
        css-class="wysiwyg"
      />
      <mj-button width="auto"
        css-class="button"
        font-size="18px"
        font-weight="700"
        font-family="Tahoma, sans-serif"
        line-height="25px"
        border-radius="100px"
        inner-padding="12px 16px"
        letter-spacing="1px"
        color="#ffffff"
        background-color="#006837"
      />
      <mj-image
        padding="0" />

      <!-- Reusable variants — apply with mj-class="*" -->
      <mj-class
        name="caption"
        font-size="10px"
        line-height="16px"
        padding="4px 0 0 0"
      />
    </mj-attributes>

    <!-- No mj-style @media blocks here: EN merges same-condition @media
         blocks into the FIRST occurrence's position (measured 2026-08-19,
         EoA delivered heads), so a 599px block in this head would drag
         styles.css's 599px rules ahead of the order-dependent hides that
         precede them there. All mobile rules live in styles.css. -->

    <mj-raw>
      <meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark" />
      <meta name="supported-color-schemes" content="light dark" />
      <meta name="format-detection" content="telephone=no" />
    </mj-raw>
    <!-- Marketing Tools builder bands. The data-en-tools-band attribute keeps
         this sheet in the template HEAD: the importer splices every other
         style element out into the Template Styles block, and this layout has
         to survive an email built without that block. __TEMPLATE_VERSION__ is
         filled in at import from versions.json; keeping the literal here is
         what stops the version from feeding its own hash.

         TWO SEPARATE RULES, DELIBERATELY NOT MERGED INTO ONE SELECTOR LIST.

         BLOCK bands hang off a span the app injects into the block's own
         content, gated on whether a block wrapper exists anywhere in the
         document — true while editing in EN, false in a delivered email.

         The TEMPLATE band hangs off EN's container element instead. A span
         authored in the template shell does NOT work (measured 2026-08-20):
         EN's builder renders blocks inside the container and does not render
         the template's own surrounding markup, so such a span never enters
         the builder DOM at all. [data-container="main"] IS that container,
         and it is self-gating — the stored template carries EN's container
         placeholder there and a delivered email carries the blocks
         themselves, so nothing bears the attribute outside the editor.

         Do NOT write EN's container merge tag literally anywhere in this
         file. autoEnableTemplateReplacements splits the shell on the FIRST
         occurrence of that tag, so a literal in a comment splits the head at
         the comment instead of at the real container: the comment loses its
         terminator, swallows the whole stylesheet, and every light/dark image
         pair renders both halves. Measured 2026-08-20; check-catalog guards it.

         Merging the two selectors would be a live hazard: one unsupported
         selector in a group invalidates the WHOLE rule, so a client that
         cannot parse :has() would take the template band down with it. -->
    <mj-raw>
      <style type="text/css" data-en-tools-band>
        /* The TEMPLATE's own band leads, because it labels the whole email
           rather than a block inside it. Order is presentational only — the
           two selectors match different elements, so nothing in the cascade
           depends on it. */
        [data-container="main"]:before{
          content: "Email Template v__TEMPLATE_VERSION__";
          display: block !important;
          font-weight: 700 !important;
          font-family: sans-serif !important;
          color: #4e535c !important;
          line-height: 1.75rem !important;
          text-align: center !important;
          margin-bottom: 2rem !important;
          background-color: #d0d5dd !important;
        }
        /* max-width tracks the EMAIL width — keep it equal to
           div[data-container]{max-width} in styles.css, or the block band
           stops centring over the column it labels. check-catalog's
           "Builder band width tracks the email width" guard enforces it. */
        :is(body):has(.en__emailbuilder__block) .marketing-tools-banner{
          display: flex !important;
          font-weight: 700 !important;
          font-family: sans-serif !important;
          color: #4e535c !important;
          line-height: 2rem !important;
          top: 1.75rem !important;
          width: 100% !important;
          max-width: 600px !important;
          justify-content: center !important;
          background-color: #d0d5dd !important;
        }
        /* position:absolute belongs to the CSS Styles Block band ALONE (user
           decision 2026-08-21). That block's real content is a <style>, so it
           has no height of its own to sit above and the band has to be lifted
           out of flow; every other band labels a block with visible content,
           where taking the label out of flow makes it overlap what it labels.
           Targeted by id rather than a new class: each band span already
           carries a unique one — #head-styles and #raw-html, from
           STYLES_BAND_ID and RAW_HTML_BAND_ID — and those ids are baked into
           the stored content of blocks already living in EN, so minting new
           ones would desync every uploaded copy. Its own rule, never merged
           into the selector list above: one unparseable selector discards the
           WHOLE rule, and this gate is exactly the kind an older engine may
           not parse (conventions.md, "Builder bands"). */
        :is(body):has(.en__emailbuilder__block) #head-styles{
          position: absolute !important;
        }
      </style>
    </mj-raw>

    <mj-include path="./styles.css" type="css" />

    <!-- Lives HERE, not in styles.css: the only block that uses it is the one
         parked below, so a rule in the shared sheet shipped ~100 delivered
         bytes of the catalog's head CSS for a block the catalog does not
         contain — against a 14,000-byte target with about 400 to spare
         (moved 2026-08-21). If this block ever returns to main.mjml, the rule
         goes back to styles.css with it.

         Why the fallback cannot be an attribute: the hero photo spans TWO
         sections, so it has to sit on the wrapper, and a wrapper cannot carry
         both a background-url and a background-color — MJML copies the colour
         onto the v:fill as color= and the Word engine paints that slab INSTEAD
         of the photo (measured 2026-08-13, EoA aBPD6k1l). !important because
         MJML writes `background: url(...)` inline as a SHORTHAND, which resets
         background-color to transparent. Behind the photo in every CSS client;
         Outlook's blocked-image behaviour is unverified (2026-08-21). -->
    <mj-style>
      .hero-photo-fallback {
        background-color: #362229 !important;
      }
    </mj-style>
  </mj-head>
  <mj-body css-class="email-body" background-color="#ffffff">
    <!-- Pulled from main.mjml on 2026-08-21 (user decision). Its photo spans
         TWO sections, so it has to sit on the wrapper — and a wrapper cannot
         carry both a background-url and a background-color, because MJML
         copies the colour onto the v:fill as color= and the Word engine paints
         that slab instead of the photo (measured 2026-08-13, EoA aBPD6k1l).
         The fallback therefore lives in styles.css as .hero-photo-fallback,
         which renders correctly in every CSS client but is UNVERIFIED in
         Outlook with images blocked: a class-based colour is not the bgcolor
         attribute Word reads most reliably, so the white heading may end up on
         nothing. That is what still needs settling — a send, read on
         outlook2021_win11_* with images blocked. -->
    <!-- START: CTA Hero (w/ Large Background Image) -->
    <!-- Fallback colour lives on the wrapper, NOT this section: MJML copies a section background-color onto the v:fill as color=, and the Word engine then paints that instead of the photo (measured 2026-08-13, EoA aBPD6k1l — variant A slabbed, B and C rendered). Behind the section it still covers a blocked image. -->
    <!-- The photo covers the HEADING section only; the logo/button row below sits on the wrapper's flat fallback colour. It used to span both, via an inner mj-wrapper — which MJML rejects, since mj-wrapper may only appear in mj-body (user decision 2026-08-21). There is no legal way to stack two painted layers across two sections: the colour cannot share a tag with the image (above), and a raw <div> outer layer would cost this block its Background Color, Padding Top/Bottom and Padding Left/Right fields, which exist only because that layer is a real mj-wrapper. Splitting the photo across both sections was the other candidate and gives each its own `cover` crop — a visible seam mid-block. -->
    <mj-wrapper css-class="hero-photo-fallback" background-url="assets/photo-river.jpg" background-size="cover" background-repeat="no-repeat" background-position="center center" padding="0">
      <!-- data-mobile-only-width was here until 2026-08-21. The 2026-08-18 audit measured this gutter INERT at desktop because the centered heading carries its own line break and never reaches it, and said to drop the flag if the copy ever wrapped at desktop. The 2026-08-21 audit sweeps three copy states including realistic length, and the control is live at BOTH viewports — so the bare "Padding Left/Right" is the honest label. -->
      <mj-section
        css-class="image-block block"
        padding="48px 32px 8px"
      >
        <mj-column>
          <mj-text data-display-toggle align="center" padding="0">
            <h1 style="color:#ffffff;">Lorem Ipsum Dolor<br />Sit Amet</h1>
          </mj-text>
        </mj-column>
      </mj-section>
      <mj-section
        css-class="image-block"
        padding="48px 32px 32px"
      >
        <mj-group>
          <mj-column vertical-align="middle" width="50%">
            <mj-image data-display-toggle css-class="light-only" src="assets/logo-tpl_color-light-green-text.png" alt="Trust for Public Land" href="https://tpl.org" width="154px" padding="0 16px 0 0" align="left" />
            <mj-raw><!--[if !mso]><!--></mj-raw>
            <mj-image css-class="dark-only" src="assets/logo-tpl_color-light-green-text.png" alt="Trust for Public Land" href="https://tpl.org" width="154px" padding="0 16px 0 0" align="left" data-style-dark-mode />
            <mj-raw><!--<![endif]--></mj-raw>
          </mj-column>
          <mj-column vertical-align="middle" width="50%">
            <mj-button data-display-toggle width="160px" href="https://tpl.org" align="right" background-color="#F7931E" padding="0" color="#ffffff">LEARN MORE</mj-button>
          </mj-column>
        </mj-group>
      </mj-section>
    </mj-wrapper>
    <!-- END: CTA Hero (w/ Large Background Image) -->
  </mj-body>
</mjml>
